**The mathematics presented** (all from the Phase A work):
- **Sign formula:** the sign of the functional equation is \((-1)^{n_-}\), where \(n_-\) is the number of eigenvalues equal to \(-\sqrt Q\).
- **Sign invariance:** permutations, inserting or deleting a pair \((-\sqrt Q,-\sqrt Q)\), and twists in even degree all preserve the sign.
- **Complete classification of those moves:** in odd degree the invariants are the core (the eigenvalues other than \(\pm\sqrt Q\)), the exact count \(n_+\) of \(+\sqrt Q\), and the parity of \(n_-\). In even degree they are the core and both parities, taken up to the twist, which negates the core and swaps the parities. The paper adds a one-line corollary of this: in a fixed odd degree the moves do nothing beyond reordering.
- **Verdict:** the conjecture holds exactly when the degree is at most 1. For every degree from 2 up, the family \([n\sqrt Q,\sqrt Q/n,\sqrt Q,\dots]\) puts infinitely many move classes inside one sign class. Systems made only of \(\pm\sqrt Q\) satisfy the conjecture in even degree but not in odd degree (\([s,s,s]\) versus \([s,-s,-s]\)).
- **Repair:** if any pair \(\{\beta,Q/\beta\}\) may be inserted or deleted, then two systems of the same degree have the same sign exactly when they are related by these moves, in every degree. There are four normal forms: \([\,]\), \([s,-s]\), \([s]\), \([-s]\).

